---
import DemoVideo from "./DemoVideo.astro";
import { acquisitionEvidence, audioSamples, capabilities, laterCWResults, matchedReplay, trialsPerLevel } from "../project-content.mjs";
const { page, locale, t, project, base, link } = Astro.props;
const reference = path => `${base}/reference/${locale.code}/${path}/`;
const media = name => `${base}/media/${name}`;
const decibels = value => value > 0 ? `+${value}` : String(value).replace("-", "−");
---
<article class="wrap section project-page">
  <h1>{project[page]}</h1>
  {locale.code !== "en" && <p class="lede">{t.ui.fallback}</p>}
  <div lang="en" dir="ltr">
    {page === "capabilities" && <>
      <p class="lede">What you can build today, how to enable each feature, and what its tests verified.</p>
      <div class="table-region" role="region" aria-label="Linkwise capabilities" tabindex="0">
        <table class="project-table capabilities-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Capability</th><th scope="col">Requirements</th><th scope="col">Tested scope</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{capabilities.map(([name, requirements, evidence, path]) => <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={reference(path)}>{name}</a></th><td>{requirements}</td><td>{evidence}</td>
          </tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <section class="project-section" id="build">
        <h2>Build from source</h2>
        <p>Install the pinned Rust toolchain and the audio build dependencies for your platform. From the repository root, build the station daemon and CLI.</p>
        <pre dir="ltr"><code>cargo build --locked --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --features audio-device --bins</code></pre>
        <p class="media-links"><a href={reference("rust/README.md")}>Development setup</a><a href={reference("rust/docs/modem-setup.md")}>Station configuration</a><a href={link("guide")}>Operator guide</a></p>
      </section>
    </>}
    {page === "demos" && <>
      <p class="lede">Watch the modem decode audio and follow the chat and mail workflows each bench demonstrated.</p>
      <nav class="jump" aria-label="Demos">
        <a href="#waveform">Waveform</a><a href="#winlink">Winlink Express</a><a href="#chat-and-mail">Chat and mail</a>
        <a href="#rocky-live-usb">Rocky Live USB</a><a href="#briefing">Study briefing</a>
      </nav>
      <section class="project-section" id="waveform">
        <h2>Watch BW500 frames decode</h2>
        <p>In this local codec loopback, the Linkwise receiver verifies three level-1 frames while the SigPlot waterfall fills. The audio samples below are generated by Linkwise.</p>
        <details class="demo-detail"><summary>Show the waveform animation</summary>
          <img class="demo-image" src={media("modem-streaming.gif")} alt="SigPlot waterfall fills as the Rust receiver verifies all three BW500 frames" loading="lazy" />
        </details>
        <div class="cards audio-samples">{audioSamples.map(([file, title, result]) => <div class="card">
          <h3>{title}</h3><p>{result}</p>
          <audio controls preload="none" src={media(file)} aria-label={title}><a href={media(file)}>{title} (WAV)</a></audio>
        </div>)}</div>
        <pre dir="ltr"><code>cargo run --locked --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --features demo --bin modem-demo</code></pre>
        <p>Open <code dir="ltr">http://127.0.0.1:8787</code> to run the interactive demo. <a href={reference("rust/docs/modem-demo.md")}>Reproduce the waveform demo</a></p>
      </section>
      <section class="project-section" id="winlink">
        <h2>Send a Winlink Express attachment</h2>
        <p>Watch two Winlink Express P2P sessions over virtual BW500 between Linkwise and stock VARA HF. Each modem initiates one session, and each peer sends and receives a 64-byte test attachment with a matching SHA-256.</p>
        <DemoVideo title="Winlink Express attachment demo" file="winlink-prbs9.mp4" poster="media/winlink-prbs9.webp" {base} />
        <div class="media-links"><a href={link("winlink")}>Set up Winlink Express</a><a href={reference("docs/media/modem/README.md")}>Fixture and verification record</a></div>
      </section>
      <section class="project-section" id="chat-and-mail">
        <h2>Connect a chat or mail client</h2>
        <div class="cards">
          <div class="card"><h3>VarAC and Linkwise chat</h3><p>Start a BW500 station and connect to a second peer. Follow the virtual audio bench for mixed VarAC and Linkwise chat, then try the station verification workflow between Linkwise peers.</p>
            <div class="media-links"><a href={link("varac")}>VarAC setup</a><a href={link("guide") + "#chat-example"}>Chat walkthrough</a></div></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Desktop and mobile mail</h3><p>Set up SMTP and IMAP with TLS against a Linkwise mailbox. The iPhone simulator test exercises Thunderbird libraries and verifies an attachment after a clean virtual BW500 exchange. Desktop and Android client evidence is recorded separately.</p>
            <div class="media-links"><a href={link("guide") + "#mail-example"}>Client walkthrough</a><a href={reference("tools/ios-thunderbird/README.md")}>Reproduce the iOS bench</a></div></div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="project-section" id="rocky-live-usb">
        <h2>Boot Rocky Live USB</h2>
        <p>Two separate ARM64 UTM guests boot hardened virtual USB media. Watch them exchange terminal chat and Open B2F mail over virtual BW500 and verify both attachment hashes.</p>
        <DemoVideo title="Rocky Live USB chat and mail demo" file="media/rocky-live-usb.mp4" poster="media/rocky-live-usb.webp" {base} />
        <h3>Compose protected mail in Mutt</h3>
        <p>Mutt sends a signed and encrypted message through Linkwise. The recipient verifies the sender and the attachment. An unknown recipient leaves the sender’s draft available for correction.</p>
        <DemoVideo title="Interactive Mutt PKI demo" file="media/rocky-interactive-mutt.mp4" poster="media/rocky-interactive-mutt.webp" {base} />
        <p class="media-links"><a href={media("rocky-mutt.mp4")}>Watch the Mutt mailbox demo</a><a href={media("rocky-pki-mail.mp4")}>Watch the protected CLI mail demo</a>
          <a href={reference("tools/rocky-live-usb/README.md")}>Build and reproduce the live media bench</a></p>
      </section>
      <section class="project-section" id="briefing">
        <h2>Linkwise modem robustness briefing</h2>
        <p>The narrated engineering briefing explains the decoder changes, the interference experiments, and how revision 0.11 of the test plan guides qualification with stock VARA.</p>
        <DemoVideo title="Linkwise modem robustness briefing" file="media/robustness-briefing.mp4" poster="media/robustness-briefing.webp" transcript="media/robustness-briefing-transcript.md" {base} />
        <a href={link("research")}>Read the results and test plan</a>
      </section>
    </>}
    {page === "research" && <>
      <p class="lede">How Linkwise measures exact payload recovery, compares against stock VARA, and qualifies changes before adoption.</p>
      <div class="media-links">
        <a href={media("modem-robustness-test-plan.pdf")}>Robustness test plan, revision 0.11 (A4 PDF)</a>
        <a href={media("lab-notes.pdf")}>Research lab notes (A4 PDF)</a><a href={link("demos") + "#briefing"}>Watch the briefing</a>
      </div>
      <section class="project-section" id="acquisition">
        <h2>Receiver changes on main</h2>
        <p>Corrected seven-bit unpacking produced identical KISS payloads to stock VARA on eleven matched receptions. The rolling level-2 pilot threshold then moved from 0.35 to 0.20. More weak candidates reach the decoder and must still pass the existing wire CRC. The transmitted waveform and framing are unchanged.</p>
        <p>On the older retained corpus at −8 dB, the acquisition change improved exact recovery from 3/20 to 15/20. The fresh paired sweep below used two distinct beacon payloads from Rockport and Kitchener. <a href={acquisitionEvidence}>Acquisition method and trial results</a></p>
        <figure class="comparison" aria-labelledby="replay-title">
          <h3 id="replay-title">Matched KiwiSDR replay against stock VARA HF 4.9.1</h3>
          <p>Exact payloads out of {trialsPerLevel} replays at each level. Each of two recordings had ten independently seeded Gaussian-noise trials per level.</p>
          <div class="table-region" role="region" aria-label="Paired replay comparison" tabindex="0">
            <table class="project-table replay-table">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Added-noise SNR</th><th scope="col">Linkwise baseline</th><th scope="col">Linkwise updated</th><th scope="col">Stock VARA HF</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>{matchedReplay.map(row => <tr>
                <th scope="row">{decibels(row.snr)} dB</th>
                {Object.entries({ baseline: row.baseline, updated: row.updated, stock: row.stock }).map(([modem, count]) =>
                  <td><strong>{count}/{trialsPerLevel}</strong><div class="replay-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class={modem} style={`width:${count / trialsPerLevel * 100}%`}></span></div></td>)}
              </tr>)}</tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <figcaption>The dB scale compares recorded burst power, including original receiver noise, with added 300–3300 Hz noise over the same burst. The results describe PCM replay. Calibrated RF sensitivity requires a known wanted signal and an independent noise measurement.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p><a href={reference("docs/testing/vara-interoperability.md")}>Two-way bench procedure and results</a></p>
      </section>
      <section class="project-section" id="interference">
        <h2>Interference experiments awaiting qualification</h2>
        <ul class="result-list">
          <li><strong>Level 2.</strong> With injected CW, the later replay improved from 8/60 to 60/60 exact payloads. The trials reused six clips representing three distinct payloads. <a href={laterCWResults}>Replay results and conditions</a></li>
          <li><strong>Level 4.</strong> The later tone-cancellation replay improved from 79/270 to 270/270 across three archived stock frames. Both paths recovered 30/30 in the original 1453 Hz, 0 dB wanted-to-interferer cell. <a href={reference("tools/soft-metric-study/results/cw-level4-2026-11-18/README.md")}>Level-4 method and evidence</a></li>
          <li><strong>Passive receive.</strong> Both paths returned identical bytes on 12 receptions from five recording windows across three sites, representing eight payload hashes. The fallback never activated. <a href={reference("tools/soft-metric-study/results/kiwisdr-passive-2026-11-18/README.md")}>Passive replay record</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Revision 0.11 defines the next tests for live impaired mixed-role sessions, real-time deadlines, false acceptance, and calibrated RF sensitivity. The interference receivers remain experimental.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="project-section" id="beacons">
        <h2>KiwiSDR beacon map and decoded fields</h2>
        <p>Kitchener received <bdi>(KW9Q, EN40KJ)</bdi> and <bdi>(N9PMI, EM78EG)</bdi> on 7.105 MHz USB. The range rings pass through the respective Maidenhead grid centers at 947 km and 718 km.</p>
        <div class="beacon-figures">
          <figure><a href={media("beacon-range-rings.png")}><img width="1240" height="877" src={media("beacon-range-rings.png")} loading="lazy" alt="Kitchener-centered range rings at 947 and 718 km through the decoded KW9Q and N9PMI grid centers" /></a><figcaption>Range rings from the collecting KiwiSDR.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><a href={media("beacon-frame-fields.png")}><img width="1240" height="877" src={media("beacon-frame-fields.png")} loading="lazy" alt="Both received byte sequences with highlighted marker, packed payload, trailer, control and CRC fields and seven-bit ASCII translations" /></a><figcaption>Framing, checksum coverage and the seven-bit hexadecimal-to-ASCII translation. The variable trailer byte remains unidentified.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <p><a href={media("kiwisdr-beacon-map-dsp.pdf")}>Download both figures (A4 PDF)</a></p>
      </section>
    </>}
  </div>
</article>
